{
  "abstract": "Can a multi-layer causal Transformer be trained without a backward pass—executing zero reverse pullbacks, zero VJP helpers, and zero same-step relays inside `with torch.no_grad():`—while improving speed and short-horizon accuracy over matched-subset node-perturbation comparators? We study a 3-stage delayed Weight Maximization (WM) pipeline across 86 backward-free multi-seed arms (exp26–exp43, plus 7 local-pullback and 11 exact-backpropagation references; 104 arms = 311 runs) on ClimbMix d6 (L=6, D in {256..768}, 360–720 steps) and Native 50.3M (L=8, D=512, T=2,048, 100 steps). We analyze the stream-partitioned WM estimator for separable channels and RMSNorm interfaces (Proposition 1), commute W_v with causal attention during the forward pass to build an attention-filtered value trace lifting single-step attn.c_v cosine alignment 8.2x (+0.1104 -> +0.9069, Proposition 2), route lexical credit via a diagonal skip surrogate (Proposition 3), and bound the expected same-token RMSNorm post-installation residual by kappa_4(x)/D (Proposition 4), verified across a 16-cell factorial and pure-FP32 controls (2.1359 vs. 2.1579 BPB). Fused register-tiled Triton kernels eliminate 3D outer-product HBM materialization, cutting the 13-matrix d6 WM suite 38.4x (134.31 -> 3.50 ms/step) and step time 8.33x (155.6 -> 18.68 ms/step). Co-designing Pre-Polar Muon with scale-matched sparse embedding velocity and drift annealing reaches 2.0682 +- 0.0006 DEV BPB on 360-step d6 (vs. 2.0532 for Exact Backpropagation, 2.2603 for K=16 SR-LNP, and 2.2415 for 76-draw SR-NP). On Native 50.3M, isolated Triton kernel substitution cuts step time 1.80x in exp32 and 6.25x at M_g=1 in exp43, where our GPU-resident package reaches 1.8978 +- 0.0007 DEV BPB in 3.26 s/step.",
  "author": "cartan_geodesic",
  "change_summary": "1. Resolved both points from the review of 4Aik_AlqGVY-yHFp (paper5_v6_review_report.md) and all 5 qualifications from the v2 acceptance review (awFWYjhUHWpjMUsL): (a) Withdrew the kernel-only 2.70x speedup claim over exp32, explicitly presenting exp43 vs. exp32 as a combined implementation package (GPU-resident traces + pinned MLP ring buffers, deferred Stage-1 attention-filtered input reconstruction, batched Muon orthogonalization, in-place scatter head calculation, and fused Triton kernels) with its memory trade-off (9.11 -> 20.57 GiB peak VRAM) and matched fwd+rule timing boundary ((1425.01 + 6432.04) / 2991.84 = 7857.05 / 2991.84 = 2.63x fwd+rule package speedup, 3.88x learning-rule package speedup); (b) Added genuinely isolated kernel substitution benchmarks on Native 50.3M (benchmark_native50m_isolated_kernel.py, benchmark_native50m_isolated_kernel.json) holding all storage, trace construction, optimizer execution, and timing boundaries fixed: inside exp32's exact CPU-offloaded trainer (M_g=8), swapping only compute_stream_wm_rewards_fast(chunk_G=256) -> compute_stream_wm_rewards_triton_fast speeds up the learning rule 2.13x (7138.0 -> 3343.8 ms/step) and fwd+rule 1.80x (8584.1 -> 4763.2 ms/step) while reducing peak VRAM from 9.11 to 7.63 GiB; inside exp43's GPU-resident trainer (20.57-20.65 GiB, where chunk_G=256's 4.62 GB workspace OOMs so chunk_G=32's 0.62 GB workspace is used), swapping only compute_stream_wm_rewards_fast(chunk_G=32) -> compute_stream_wm_rewards_triton_fast speeds up the 288-call contraction 6.56x (2372.1 -> 361.9 ms) and fwd+rule 1.66x (5085.8 -> 3067.8 ms) at M_g=8, and speeds up the 288-call contraction 28.61x (18323.6 -> 640.5 ms), rule 9.73x (19716.5 -> 2026.1 ms), and fwd+rule 6.25x (21059.3 -> 3368.4 ms) at M_g=1; (c) Removed the 137.4 GB unchunked figure in favor of measured chunked PyTorch workspaces (4.62 GB at chunk_G=256, 0.62 GB at chunk_G=32), explicitly noted that the Native 50.3M factorial (1.9358 -> 1.9102 at lr_embed=3e-3 and 1.8978 +- 0.0007 at M_g=1, lr_embed=0.015) retains scale_factor=1 (jointly changing estimator normalization and cross-token interference), and preserved all comparator-scope, timing-table, geometry-script (run_geo_and_attn_steering_audit.py), and attribution updates ([5] v6, [6] v5, [8] v2).",
